# Buddy auth and secrets

Human guide: `docs/auth.md`. Session pick is `ResolveSession` in `internal/buddy/session.go`.

- Config is `tenant:` (`default`) + `tenants:` + `auths:` + optional `channels:` policies. Older `credentials:` / string `tenant:` / `defaults:` / `allow_auths:` are rejected. Selection: `--tenant` (auto-pick) or `--user`/`--auth` (pin). Auth `channels:` limits buddy clients `cli|mcp|ide` (omit = all). Top-level `channels.<client>` sets `access` / `tenant` / `tenants` allowlist, optional `deny_builtins`, and optional `allow_builtins` (allow follows deny). The same deny/allow lists are valid on `tenants.` and `auths.`; layers are independent (channel allow cannot unblock a tenant deny). MCP always denies `jamf_*` on the channel layer unless `allow_builtins` unblocks it. `deny_builtins: ["*"]` is an allowlist at that layer. Cowboy channel override: `allow_builtins: ["*"]`.
- macOS org defaults: Jamf ACS/MCX preference domain `virtualpete.buddy.cli` (or `BUDDY_MANAGED_PLIST`) overlays onto user `config.yaml` (user wins per name). Auths in the plist are accepted if present; `buddy setup managed-plist` omits them. Prefer `buddy setup secrets` for the store. `SaveConfig` does not snapshot unmodified MDM keys.
- Token logic lives in `internal/buddy/tokenauth/`; process cache in `common.go` (`credAuthCache` keyed `auth@tenant`); disk cache in `~/.config/buddy/tokens/` (AES-GCM; Darwin wraps with Keychain account `token-wrap`, no userPresence / not the vault DEK; Linux uses compiled-in obfuscation; skipped for command auth). Debug/`--trace`/`--script-trace` go through `internal/redact` (JWTs, Bearer, `access_token`/`token`/`client_secret`); `auth token show --raw` is the explicit leak.
- Secrets: inline config wins, then `secrets.DefaultStore()` (encrypted `secrets.enc` vault; random DEK in Keychain on macOS / `dek` file on Linux; recovery passphrase in Keychain `master` or `master` file). `BUDDY_VAULT_PASSPHRASE` overrides the recovery passphrase (never written; `dek:v1:…` overrides the DEK). Signed Darwin (`-tags secitem`): unlock once per process into `VaultStore` session (`UnlockSession` at mcp/serve start; CLI lazy on first vault use). Prefer Keychain `userPresence` when the platform allows it; bare Developer ID CLI falls back to LocalAuthentication `evaluatePolicy` (SecAccessControl needs restricted keychain entitlements / `.app`). Escape: `BUDDY_VAULT_UNLOCK=after-first-unlock`. Linux / Windows / unsigned `./buddy` stay file/`security(1)` without presence. No PBKDF2 on ordinary vault decrypt.
- Resolve via `CredentialConfig.ResolveAuthWithSecrets` (composed tenant+auth view) before building an `Authenticator`.
- CLI: `buddy auth token show|token status|token drop|token refresh|test|authorization`, `buddy token direct`, `buddy cred status|add`, `buddy cred set tenant|auth|channel|secret|passphrase …` (`--channels=`), `buddy cred delete tenant|auth|secret …` (cascade + store secrets), `buddy cred clean secrets|tokens`, `buddy cred erase secrets`, `buddy cred backup|restore <file>`, `buddy cred use [--cli|--mcp|--ide]`, `buddy cred store`, `buddy cred migrate`.
- Token refresh: reuse until expiry (minus ~15s skew). Keep-alive is **basic auth only** on 401 or `buddy auth token refresh` when a cached token is still usable (otherwise obtain). OAuth/platform `auth token refresh` replaces via client credentials (also on expiry or 401). Authenticated HTTP retries once on 401 after refresh. Disk token files are purged when expired (`cred clean tokens` or on load miss); `auth token drop` invalidates still-valid cache files. CLI loads vault secrets lazily only when minting/refreshing a token (valid disk cache skips Touch ID); mcp/serve still unlock the vault session at start.
- Live checks: `buddy auth test` always ForceRenews (instance and platform). Probe 403 after a minted token is `live=token-ok`, not a bad secret. Last results + Jamf authorization details (`GET /api/v1/auth`) are cached under `~/.config/buddy/caps/` and used by session pick (fail open). `cred get secret` is presence only.
- buddyscript/MCP session kwargs are `tenant?` / `surface?` / `auth?` (`cred` removed). Soft-switch on auth pin alone is a fallback when the implicit tenant is not linked. A readonly pin falls back to a full-access auth on the same tenant for mutates; channel `access: readonly` does not. Classic follows a platform pin by default (`/api/proclassic/tenant/{id}/…` with `/JSSResource` stripped). Set `features.classic_instance_only: true` or `BUDDY_CLASSIC_INSTANCE_ONLY=true` to force Classic onto instance. That toggle is Classic `/JSSResource` only. ACS `/api/config-profiles/` and undocumented Pro `GET /api/v1/policies` are always instance (Jamf Pro UI / unpublished JSON; not on the gateway).
